# DISABLED 2026-08-25 by owner decision — renamed to `.disabled` so GitHub does
# not schedule it. Nothing here is broken: run by hand it PASSES, and it is the
# only check that can see a GHSA-only advisory at all.
#
# Why it was switched off rather than fixed: it needs a `DEPENDABOT_ALERTS_TOKEN`
# secret, because `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot read the Dependabot alerts endpoint and
# `permissions: security-events: read` does not cover it. Without the secret it
# exits 2 (CANNOT MEASURE) on every run — correct behaviour, and a permanently
# red check is one everybody learns to walk past, which is worse than no check.
#
# The gate script `scripts/dependabot-alert-gate.sh` is UNCHANGED and still works:
#
#     ./scripts/dependabot-alert-gate.sh --severity medium
#
# To re-enable: add the secret, then `git mv` this file back to `.yml`.
#
# WHAT IS LOST WHILE THIS IS OFF, stated plainly so nobody rediscovers it the
# hard way: `cargo deny check advisories` answers "is anything here listed in
# RustSec", NOT "is anything here known-vulnerable". It printed `advisories ok`
# on a tree carrying an open moderate alert, because RustSec had 1,235
# advisories and no thrift entry while GitHub's superset did. That blind spot is
# structural and has already recurred twice on the same crate. Cross-check
# Dependabot by hand before a release.

# Security advisories — TWO sources, because neither one is complete.
#
# paiml-mcp-agent-toolkit#1074.
#
#   `cargo deny check advisories` answers "is anything here listed in RustSec".
#   CI reads that as "is anything here known-vulnerable". Those are different
#   questions, and GitHub's advisory database is a strict superset of RustSec —
#   it ingests RustSec, plus GHSA-native advisories, plus CVEs mapped to crates
#   that were never filed with RustSec at all.
#
#   Live, twice, on the same crate: GHSA-2f9f-gq7v-9h6m / CVE-2026-43868 in
#   `thrift 0.17.0`. Dependabot calls it an open moderate alert on this
#   repository. `cargo deny check advisories` prints `advisories ok` and exits 0,
#   because there is no thrift entry among RustSec's 1,235 advisories. The first
#   occurrence was worked around by hand and nothing was added to CI, so it came
#   back.
#
#   So the gate that could block a merge was structurally blind, and reported the
#   blind spot as a pass. This workflow is the second source.
#
# IT DOES NOT REPLACE cargo-deny, which still runs (blocking, all four families)
# in quality-gate.yml. Neither source subsumes the other: RustSec carries
# unmaintained / yanked / unsound findings that Dependabot does not model at all,
# and Dependabot carries GHSA-native advisories RustSec has never heard of. Both
# run. Both block.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT YET A REQUIRED STATUS CHECK, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
#
# The required contexts are listed in .github/required-status-checks.txt:
#   ci / gate, feature-gate, docs build (docs.rs environment), pmat score,
#   provable ladder
#
# `dependabot-alerts` is not among them yet. The quality-gate.yml comment this
# workflow replaces stated the precondition plainly: the gate "needs a token
# permission this workflow has never exercised, and a gate whose API call fails
# must fail the build rather than skip, so it has to be proven working before it
# guards a required check." That precondition is still unmet at the time of
# writing — whether the default GITHUB_TOKEN can read /dependabot/alerts was NOT
# verified, because it can only be verified by running this workflow. The gate
# was proven against the live API with a user PAT, and against fixtures; the
# GITHUB_TOKEN path was not.
#
# This repository has already been bitten by getting that order wrong: an agent
# added `mutation-diff` to live branch protection while its workflow existed
# only on a feature branch, making every pull request permanently unmergeable,
# including the one that would have landed the workflow. See the long note in
# required-status-checks.txt.
#
# PROMOTION, once one run of this job has been observed green on master:
#
#   gh api -X PATCH \
#     repos/paiml/paiml-mcp-agent-toolkit/branches/master/protection/required_status_checks \
#     --input - <<'JSON'
#   {"strict": true, "contexts": ["ci / gate","feature-gate","docs build (docs.rs environment)","pmat score","provable ladder","dependabot-alerts"]}
#   JSON
#
# and add `dependabot-alerts` to .github/required-status-checks.txt in the SAME
# change — CB-2100 fails on any disagreement between that manifest and live
# protection, which is exactly the drift it exists to catch.
#
# If the job's first run fails with "CANNOT MEASURE ... 403", the token is the
# cause, not the gate: mint a fine-grained PAT scoped to this repository with
# `Dependabot alerts: read`, store it as the DEPENDABOT_ALERTS_TOKEN secret, and
# re-run. The job already prefers that secret over GITHUB_TOKEN.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

name: Security Advisories

on:
  push:
    branches: [main, master]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main, master]
  # A new GHSA can be published against a pin that has not changed in months, so
  # this has to run on a clock and not only on a diff. That is precisely how the
  # thrift advisory appeared: the lockfile did not move, the advisory database
  # did.
  schedule:
    - cron: "17 5 * * *"
  workflow_dispatch:

concurrency:
  group: security-advisories-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

permissions:
  contents: read
  # Documented as covering Dependabot alerts. UNVERIFIED on this endpoint — see
  # the promotion note above; if it is insufficient the job fails loudly with a
  # 403 rather than passing, which is the entire design.
  security-events: read

jobs:
  dependabot-alerts:
    name: dependabot-alerts
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7

      # Runs BEFORE the live measurement, on purpose. If the gate itself is
      # broken, that must surface even on a day when the API is unreachable —
      # otherwise the first thing anyone learns about a broken gate is that it
      # went quiet. 16 cases: a known-vulnerable finding must go red, the same
      # finding under a live acknowledgement must go green, and every
      # cannot-measure path must exit 2. It includes counter-tests — an empty
      # list, a below-threshold finding and an already-fixed alert must all PASS
      # — so "refuse everything" cannot be mistaken for a working gate.
      #
      # No `|| true`, no `continue-on-error`. If this step is ever made
      # non-blocking, the gate has been deleted without anyone deleting it.
      - name: Prove the gate can fail (self-test)
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.DEPENDABOT_ALERTS_TOKEN || secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        run: ./scripts/dependabot-alert-gate.sh --self-test

      # The measurement. Exit 1 = a finding blocks. Exit 2 = the answer could not
      # be established, which fails too — "we could not measure it" must never
      # render as "nothing was found". That is the defect being fixed here, so
      # reproducing it in the fix would be its own punchline.
      - name: Dependabot advisory gate (blocking)
        env:
          # A fine-grained PAT with `Dependabot alerts: read` if one has been
          # provisioned; otherwise the workflow token. An absent secret is an
          # empty string, so the `||` falls through.
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.DEPENDABOT_ALERTS_TOKEN || secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          GITHUB_REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}
        run: |
          # NOT `gate.sh | tee gate.log`: a pipeline reports the LAST command's
          # status, so without pipefail `tee` would swallow every failure and the
          # step would be green whatever the gate found. Written out longhand so
          # the exit code is the gate's own, and so the 1-vs-2 distinction
          # survives into the log.
          set +e
          ./scripts/dependabot-alert-gate.sh --severity medium > gate.log 2>&1
          rc=$?
          set -e
          cat gate.log
          echo "gate exit code: $rc  (0 = clean, 1 = blocking finding, 2 = COULD NOT MEASURE)"
          exit "$rc"

      - name: Summary
        if: always()
        run: |
          {
            cat <<'MD'
          ### Dependabot advisory gate

          The second advisory source, alongside `cargo deny check` in quality-gate.yml.
          GitHub's advisory database is a superset of RustSec, so a green cargo-deny is
          not evidence that nothing is known-vulnerable (#1074).

          ```
          MD
            if [ -f gate.log ]; then cat gate.log; else echo "(no log — the gate step did not run)"; fi
            cat <<'MD'
          ```

          Accepted findings, each with an owner and an expiry date:
          `.github/dependabot-acknowledgements.toml`
          MD
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
